+
+This interface emits one JSON document with `format: limine-native-description`, `schema: 1` and `scope: native-operation`. It reports native configuration and input observations, prospective destinations and command line, and matching current-OS/direct-kernel candidates with their original configuration lines. Naming and association rules are shared with native publication.
+
+`--describe` must be first. The route bypasses authentication, terminal setup, locks and hooks. Mutation controls are not accepted as operands; use an explicit lexical path such as `./--quiet` when that is a filename. Inputs and outputs may be absent, and an absent `limine.conf` is reported without creating it. Unresolved identity, required read errors and ambiguous associations return an incomplete observation. Ordinary input/config symlinks are followed and identified; source basenames are taken from the supplied path, not its symlink target.
+
+Exit 0 with `complete: true` means the requested description was obtained. Exit 2 is an invalid request; exit 1 is incomplete observation. A complete description can include known missing resources or an explicitly configured ESP that fails the separately reported `mutation_preconditions.esp_vfat` check. It is not permission to mutate or proof of package coverage, publication, signing or bootability.
+
+Configuration/entry observations are **as read**, bound to their captured input identities and hashes. They are not a locked filesystem snapshot; consumers must revalidate them at their own transaction boundary. `expected.new_entry_default_name` is the default for creating an entry; an existing matching entry retains its observed display name. Raw lines are not a complete interpretation of Limine URIs. Hash-tool availability depends on the reported process PATH and needs a fresh check when using that policy. No producer-issued filesystem initialization, logging or mutation is performed; normal filesystem access-time behavior is not suppressed.
+
+The interface describes an explicit native operation. Package-declared kernel inventory, shell build-mode/fallback selection and actual producer completion remain with their respective owners.
